WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.287
A useful schema describes the smallest
shape an operation needs.

2
00:00:04.287 --> 00:00:07.172
Start with whether the top level must be

3
00:00:07.172 --> 00:00:09.968
an object, then specify each accepted
field.

4
00:00:09.968 --> 00:00:12.853
Required, optional, and nullable mean
different things.

5
00:00:12.853 --> 00:00:15.738
A missing field can mean leave the
existing

6
00:00:15.738 --> 00:00:18.894
value unchanged, while null can mean clear
it.

7
00:00:18.894 --> 00:00:22.591
Define those meanings deliberately, and
reject combinations the operation does

8
00:00:22.591 --> 00:00:26.378
not support rather than relying on later
code to guess.

9
00:00:26.378 --> 00:00:29.083
Value constraints make the shape
operationally meaningful.

10
00:00:29.083 --> 00:00:32.059
Bound string lengths, require finite
numbers within appropriate

11
00:00:32.059 --> 00:00:35.214
ranges, and distinguish integer counts
from fractional measurements.

12
00:00:35.214 --> 00:00:37.378
Use explicit booleans and closed
enumerations

13
00:00:37.378 --> 00:00:39.362
where the contract requires them.

14
00:00:39.362 --> 00:00:41.977
Specify identifier and date
representations, including which

15
00:00:41.977 --> 00:00:44.682
format checks the chosen validator
actually enforces.

16
00:00:44.682 --> 00:00:47.387
Constrain both array length and each
element.

17
00:00:47.387 --> 00:00:50.453
Apply object rules recursively, and bound
dictionary keys,

18
00:00:50.453 --> 00:00:53.338
value shapes, property counts, and
permitted nesting.

19
00:00:53.338 --> 00:00:56.674
For a profile update, the public contract
might allow

20
00:00:56.674 --> 00:01:00.281
an optional display name of one to one
hundred characters

21
00:01:00.281 --> 00:01:03.707
and an optional timezone from the
application's supported set.

22
00:01:03.707 --> 00:01:06.412
Decide whether an empty update is
meaningful.

23
00:01:06.412 --> 00:01:10.019
Neither a role nor a tenant identifier
belongs in that

24
00:01:10.019 --> 00:01:13.445
contract simply because the persistence
entity has those fields.

25
00:01:13.445 --> 00:01:17.142
Strictness means making the acceptance
policy explicit; it does not

26
00:01:17.142 --> 00:01:20.569
require every interface to make the same
compatibility decision.

27
00:01:20.569 --> 00:01:24.356
Unknown properties need a deliberate
policy at every relevant level.

28
00:01:24.356 --> 00:01:26.339
Rejecting them detects contract mistakes.

29
00:01:26.339 --> 00:01:28.503
Stripping them can support compatible
clients

30
00:01:28.503 --> 00:01:31.208
if the application uses the stripped
result.

31
00:01:31.208 --> 00:01:33.372
A constrained extension area can support

32
00:01:33.372 --> 00:01:35.717
metadata without opening the entire
object.

33
00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:37.971
Defaults vary between validators, so
verify

34
00:01:37.971 --> 00:01:40.315
the selected behavior with nested
examples.

35
00:01:40.315 --> 00:01:43.201
Always continue with the value returned by
validation

36
00:01:43.201 --> 00:01:46.627
when the validator returns an accepted or
transformed object.

37
00:01:46.627 --> 00:01:49.873
Calling a schema parser and then updating
the database

38
00:01:49.873 --> 00:01:53.299
with the original request body discards
the boundary's result.

39
00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:56.275
Downstream code must receive the contract
you checked,

40
00:01:56.275 --> 00:01:58.980
not another copy of the unverified input.

